{
  "id": "longest-path-in-dag",
  "questionId": "longest-path-in-dag",
  "slug": "longest-path-in-dag",
  "title": "Longest Path in a DAG",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Topological sort",
  "patterns": [
    "topological sort",
    "graph",
    "dag",
    "dynamic programming"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "the longest path in a DAG can be found without exploring every path individually, if you process nodes in an order where all of a node's predecessors are handled first - what ordering guarantees that?",
    "get a topological order of the nodes (Kahn's algorithm works well here)",
    "keep a dist[] array (longest path ending at each node), initialized to 0",
    "process nodes in topological order; for each outgoing edge u -> v, try to improve dist[v] = max(dist[v], dist[u] + 1)",
    "the answer is the maximum value in dist[] after processing every node"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "computing the longest path by naive DFS without memoization or topological ordering, causing exponential blowup on dense DAGs",
    "returning the number of nodes on the path instead of the number of edges (off-by-one)",
    "processing an edge before both of its endpoints' topological positions are settled, causing an under-count"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a directed acyclic graph (DAG) with n nodes, labeled 0 to\nn - 1, and a list of directed edges, where each edge `[u, v]` means\nthere is an edge from node u to node v.\n\n**Return the length (measured in number of edges) of the longest path\nthat can be formed by following directed edges through the graph.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= n <= 10^4",
      "each edge [u, v] has 0 <= u, v < n",
      "the graph is guaranteed to have no cycles"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a graph with no edges at all - the answer is 0",
      "a single node graph",
      "a DAG with diverging and converging paths, where the longest path isn't the one with the most edges out of node 0 specifically",
      "several disconnected components - the answer is the maximum across all of them"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 5, edges = [[0, 1], [1, 2], [2, 3], [0, 3], [3, 4]]",
        "output": "4",
        "expectedOutput": "4",
        "explanation": "the path 0 -> 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4 uses 4 edges, longer than the direct 0 -> 3 -> 4 shortcut"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 4, edges = []",
        "output": "0",
        "expectedOutput": "0",
        "explanation": "with no edges, the longest possible path is a single node, using 0 edges"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 6, edges = [[0, 1], [0, 2], [1, 3], [2, 3], [3, 4], [3, 5]]",
        "output": "3",
        "expectedOutput": "3",
        "explanation": "both 0 -> 1 -> 3 -> 4 and 0 -> 2 -> 3 -> 5 use 3 edges, and nothing does better"
      }
    ],
